WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.814
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.814 --> 00:00:07.730
MOHAN KRISHNAN: Hello!

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.560
Good morning, ladies
and gentlemen.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.840
My name is Mohan Krishnan.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.970
And I'm the CTO at BBM.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.590
Today, I want to
share with you guys

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:18.659
a story about how we
migrated the BBM users--

00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:20.950
and this is what was originally
known as the BlackBerry

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.900
Messenger-- from our Canadian
data center, on-prem,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.880
to Google Cloud.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.040
Before starting though,
I'd like to ask maybe

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.000
you guys a couple of questions.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.830
Firstly, how many of you all
are considering doing migration

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.650
from on-prem to the cloud
in maybe the next six

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.090
to nine months?

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.490
Show of hands?

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:43.380
OK, great.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.250
So hopefully this content
is relevant for you all.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.580
The next question, how many
of you all know what BBM is?

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.390
OK, keep your hands up.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.390
How many of you all
know that BBM is today

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:59.070
available on Android and iOS
and continuously being improved?

00:00:59.070 --> 00:00:59.760
Great!

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.490
So you guys know our story.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.120
That's awesome.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:03.953
Thank you very much.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.035
So let's start.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:11.160
What I'd like to start with
is a bit of background,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.650
because I get lots of questions
as to what's going on with BBM?

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:15.790
What's happening?

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.510
So this is just to
set things up a bit.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.460
I work for a company
called Emtek.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.610
Emtek is Indonesia's
largest media conglomerate.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.660
We own TV stations, both paid
and free-to-air national TV

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.950
stations.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.280
We own production houses and
been in the media business

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.860
for about 5 to 10 years.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.640
In about the last
two to three years,

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.140
we've also been going
online, primarily, again,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.480
taking our content from
traditional media to online.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:48.690
So we run, today, Indonesia's
largest online news portals,

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.000
similar to like Conde
Nast but for the internet.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.920
We also run Indonesia's
second largest video platform.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.340
We're only second to YouTube.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.050
Today, our content
and our services

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.410
touch over 50 to 60 million
monthly active users

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.520
in Indonesia.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.639
As part of that
strategy, of growing

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.280
that side of the
business, we decided

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.760
that, besides just being
a content producer,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.390
we also wanted to be a platform,
a platform for people to come

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.310
and consume our content.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.370
To that goal, we found
that in Indonesia there's

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.040
still a large
amount of BBM users

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.940
compared to the
rest of the world.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.010
And we then engaged with
BlackBerry to wholly license--

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.500
so we've acquired a
license from BlackBerry--

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.750
for BBM for the
consumer business.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.320
So today, since 2016, when
the agreement was signed,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.570
we build, develop,
and distribute

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.900
BBM for the consumer market.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.870
A key part of achieving
our goals around that

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.550
was migrating BBM.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.850
And I'm going to get into the
specifics around that in a bit.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:58.680
But I'd like to share how
large, from a human standpoint,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:02:59.990
this project was.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.340
It spanned about two years.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.210
We officially only
finished in June 2018.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.380
It involved anywhere
between 40 to 60 engineers,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.320
spanned across
12-hour time zones,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.370
across three
different countries,

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.245
Canada, Singapore, and Jakarta.

00:03:19.245 --> 00:03:21.120
The next thing I want
to share with you guys,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.490
before we get to the
specifics, is that this is not

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.150
your father's BBM anymore.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.320
Many people still think that
BBM is only available on BBOS

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.010
and BB10 devices.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.290
This is BBM for Android and
iOS, modern operating systems.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.870
Apart from that,
this is also a BBM

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.870
that is on par with
your modern Facebook

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.970
Messengers and WhatsApp.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.970
This includes things like
video and voice calling,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.750
large groups, availability on
multiple platforms including

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.890
desktop.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.440
And more importantly,
what we've been focused on

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.570
and why we made
this transition is

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.840
we're building features
for our market.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.010
How many of you all are
familiar with WeChat?

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.420
So OK, WeChat, you
guys know that story.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.200
It's huge in China.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.050
And the reason why
it's huge, also,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.860
is because it's a messenger
that's focused for that market.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.310
That's exactly what we're doing.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.810
We're trying to make BBM
the WeChat of Indonesia.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.890
To that goal, we've been
building features like wallets.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.120
So today, if you
use BBM Indonesia,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.680
you can send money
to each other.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.510
You can make transactions.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.720
You can buy things
like internet pop ups

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.580
or pay your phone bills.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.580
Apart from that, we've
also been integrating BBM

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.984
into the local phone systems.

00:04:34.984 --> 00:04:36.900
In the next two months,
we will be rolling out

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.320
a feature where you can
make calls and receive

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.980
calls via a number that
is assigned to your BBM.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.350
So when you register
for BBM in Indonesia,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.860
you get a local phone number.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:48.900
And you can use it.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.790
This is extremely
relevant for the market

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.530
that we operate
where there are still

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.720
many users, who
are in rural areas,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.880
with very poor
data connectivity.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.250
They can still keep in
touch by piggybacking

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.950
on the PSTN network to
contact their friends

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.730
and family on BBM.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:09.820
So in a nutshell, what
was the transition?

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.960
So it involved moving the
BBM infrastructure footprint

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.910
from Canada all the way to
GCP in the Asian region.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.060
And why did we do this?

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.710
Well, first and foremost,
as you guys probably know,

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.010
the global user base
for BBM has reduced.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:33.070
But there's still a
sizable user base in Asia,

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:37.750
primarily in Indonesia, in
Africa, and in the Middle East.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.120
We wanted to bring
the BBM services

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:41.950
closer to where our users are.

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.580
This will improve latency.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.550
It would also improve
network reliability.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:46.920
That was the first goal.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.710
But a second major driving
goal was replatforming

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.410
BBM on cloud
infrastructure that'll

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.420
allow us to build
the type of features,

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:56.880
that I was talking
about earlier,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.990
in a fast and effective manner.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:00.940
That was a huge goal.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:02.650
Lastly, it's important
to keep in mind

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:06.170
that, during the entire 1 and
1/2 years that we were doing

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.700
this transition, we still
had billions of messages

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:13.450
and tens of millions of
users using the platform

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.280
without any problem.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.200
We were migrating them,
in the background,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.870
without them realizing, and
not impacting their service.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.570
Also, during these 18
months, we continued

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:30.190
to push out new and exciting
features for our user base.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.662
So what was it that we migrated?

00:06:32.662 --> 00:06:34.120
I know there are
some ex BlackBerry

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.470
people in the audience today.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.590
So these guys are
probably familiar with it.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:45.220
But BBM was not a simple
application to migrate.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.870
It consisted of over
20 major components.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:53.260
Each of these components
had multiple subcomponents.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:56.270
You look at it and
you might go, wow.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.900
It looks like a
microservices architecture.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.810
From our end, I think it was
an accidental microservice

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:02.800
architecture.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.190
It was an amalgamation of
different components, built

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.720
over a 10 to 3 year time
line, from different teams,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.210
using different
technologies and frameworks

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.040
that were available
at that point.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.010
That added a lot of complexity.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:21.790
We had everything from JBoss,
different version of Spring,

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.910
custom Java frameworks
that we had to rationalize

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.180
and plan how we were going
to deploy and run in GCP.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.120
Apart from that, it was
a sizable infrastructure

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.070
footprint, over 14,000 VMs,
on physical machines running

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:41.080
in BlackBerry's data centers
in Canada, NetApp appliances,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:48.250
Fusion-io, fiber-based
storage, a large data

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.190
analytics and Hadoop
clusters, and also

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.820
an assortment of databases.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.940
We had to migrate a
600 node Cassandra

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:03.530
cluster, multiple mySQL and
Postgres databases, as well.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:08.570
So it was a large piece
of infrastructure to move.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.450
This diagram here is
mostly for shock factor.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:12.950
Don't look at it too hard.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.680
But this is an attempt
at trying to map out

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.520
all the different
components that made up

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.090
BBM and their interconnection.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.370
As you can see,
the summary is it

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.030
wasn't a simple system,
neatly compartmentalized

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:27.590
into logical components.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.990
That, it was not.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:32.210
So how did we do the migration?

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.799
What did it involve?

00:08:33.799 --> 00:08:36.770
There were three
major parts or tasks

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.120
that we had to go through.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.520
Firstly, was the preparation.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.710
And I'll talk about
that in a bit.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.530
Secondly, we mapped out
the different services

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:49.910
that were running and how they
would end up running in GCP.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.610
And lastly was the
actual traffic cutover.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:56.440
And I'll go through
that one by one.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.710
What we were trying to
answer in preparation was

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:05.160
what is there to move and
where do we move it to?

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.280
The reason why we had this
question, of what is that move,

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.860
was, keep in mind,
that the team that

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.310
was going to do this
migration, for the most part,

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.910
did not build and did
not operate the service.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.450
They didn't have any idea
as to what was running.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.880
So we had to go
through a process

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.220
of trying to
inventory the service

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.260
and understand what
was there first.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.680
From an inventory standpoint,
there were two major aspects.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.360
Firstly, was just mapping
out the individual services.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.200
Where is the source
code for this service?

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.130
How do you build it?

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.690
What are the Jenkins pipelines?

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.280
What are the dependencies?

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.520
What are the configurations
that we need to pull in?

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.310
Each component had
anywhere between 400 to 500

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.150
different configurations.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.640
At times, there
were configurations

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.350
where people didn't understand.

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.810
It's just been that
way for multiple years.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.810
And we had to do a lot
of so-called archeology

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.090
to try to figure out and
put the pieces together.

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.790
And the second part
of the inventory

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.830
was from an
operations standpoint.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.730
How did these services run
in their current on-prem

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:10.020
infrastructure?

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.850
What were the type
of service that were

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.800
required to run these services?

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.720
What were the type of
metrics and alerting

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.450
that was used to make sure that
these services were healthy?

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.090
What type of log output
did they produce?

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.220
Which parts of the
log were relevant

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.170
from an operational standpoint?

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.810
And lastly, even
bandwidth utilization,

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.340
because we were
moving to a model

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:33.060
where egress bandwidth
was going to be needed,

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.460
as opposed to a
typical on-prem setup,

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.240
where you buy the bulk,
top, peak bandwidth

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.630
throughput that you need.

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:44.210
So we had to map that
out and figure it out.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:47.360
What worked really well for
us, from a service standpoint

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:49.520
of trying to figure out
how the service ran,

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.220
is that we documented
everything in code.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.090
We didn't just
write it all down.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:56.720
We actually had our
developers work-out,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.615
write Ansible playbooks
to get this all set up.

00:10:59.615 --> 00:11:01.490
And they made sure that
the services at least

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.740
stood up within a Vagrant box.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.870
That gave us the assuredness
that this information

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:07.310
was documented.

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:09.530
From an operation
standpoint, what we did was

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:13.070
we created this
giant spreadsheet

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:13.980
as you can see here.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:15.470
Again, don't look
at it too hard.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:18.890
But essentially, it mapped
out all different aspects

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:22.900
of the service and how
it ran in production.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.750
The next step was
actually deciding

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.340
where we were going to go.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:29.490
It wasn't a slam dunk that
we were moving to GCP.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.180
We actually ran POCs with
three different cloud vendors.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.610
I'm sure Tim and some of the
guys from Google in the crowd

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:36.990
still remember this.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.160
We ran two to four week POCs
on site with BlackBerry,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:45.150
where what we did was
we tried to put one,

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.620
two key services into the
cloud and then actually load

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.810
test it in there,
see how it worked.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.230
This gave us two things.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.092
Firstly, it helped
us further understand

00:11:55.092 --> 00:11:57.300
these individual services,
because we were also still

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:58.350
figuring it out.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.200
And secondly, it gave
us a lot more clarity

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.150
as to how the
services were running

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.870
within the individual
cloud providers.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.970
In the end, we
did an evaluation.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.880
We evaluated on
three major criteria,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:15.570
the technical capabilities of
the cloud provider, its partner

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:17.790
support, and costs.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.010
And it goes without
saying, I'm here at Next,

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:22.450
and GCP was the winner.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:25.012
And we've been happy
with that decision.

00:12:25.012 --> 00:12:26.470
We've been happy
with the decision.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:27.930
It's really worked out for us.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.630
From a partner
standpoint, as well,

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:31.380
we worked with some
really great partners,

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.170
like Pythian and CloudCover,
who are also in the room here

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:34.990
today.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:38.250
And that really got
us over the line.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:41.160
The table on the right,
here, shows some--

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.790
like, in a condensed form--
the different aspects

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:44.940
of the comparison.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:46.350
There were a lot more details.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.540
I can get into specifics,
if you guys want,

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.130
but it was a long process.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:52.480
By the time we were
done with this phase,

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.950
inventorying the system and
picking the cloud vendor,

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:00.060
we were four months into
this project already.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.520
So now that we know
what we want to migrate,

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.620
we know where we're
going to migrate to,

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.260
the next step was to
really figure out,

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:12.446
how are we going to have these
components run in the cloud?

00:13:12.446 --> 00:13:13.820
And the first
question-- and I've

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.862
seen this question being
repeated through conversations

00:13:16.862 --> 00:13:18.320
that I've had with
other people who

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:19.430
have gone through migration.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.929
And I'm sure some of you guys,
who are considering migration

00:13:21.929 --> 00:13:24.980
now, are having this
exact question--

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:28.010
is do we just lift
and shift the services

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:32.150
as is or do we use this as an
opportunity to re-engineer it,

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.550
replatform it on something new?

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:38.390
Now intuitively, lifting
and shifting makes sense.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:39.420
You minimize risk.

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.780
You're already making
this major change.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.030
Why change the application
at the same time?

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.820
Why increase the complexity?

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:50.990
But the challenge with
services that were originally

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.050
built for on-prem
infrastructure, I believe,

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:56.120
is that sometimes there
are certain aspects

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:59.270
or certain operational
aspects of those services

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:03.980
that rely on very specific
on-prem characteristics.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:06.800
Trying to replicate these
on-prem characteristics

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:11.310
in the cloud end up adding
risk to your project.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:13.040
So I don't think
it's a done deal.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.650
I don't think
anybody, who's making

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.650
a large migration, a
complex enough migration,

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:23.510
is ever going to have purely a
lift and shift or a replatform,

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:24.350
re-engineer.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:27.170
It's going to be a
spectrum, a hybrid approach.

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:30.200
And for us, that's
exactly what happened.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.170
There were certain
characteristics of components

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:36.650
that, when we saw, we realized
that we'd have to re-engineer.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:40.970
The first being applications
that relied on file storage.

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:44.540
So these were applications
that wrote to disk,

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.700
either the SanDisks on NetApps.

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:50.060
We realized, early on, that
there was a great opportunity

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.000
to move this to cloud
storage, which was more

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:54.710
suited for object storage.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:58.524
Now, making this change
was a lot of work.

00:14:58.524 --> 00:14:59.690
It involved a lot of change.

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:02.100
We practically rewrote
these components.

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.670
However, what we realized,
in rewriting them,

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.160
there was a lot of existing
complexity within these

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.340
applications-- for example,
to handle sharding between

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:14.240
multiple file end points or
to handle failures that only

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:16.430
happen within file systems--

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:17.650
we could remove.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.360
And it actually greatly
simplified these applications.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.490
Yes, we had to
introduce special code

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:25.700
to handle the fact that now
your writes have much higher

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:28.310
latency, because they're going
over HTTP, but, in the end,

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:30.390
we felt the
trade-off made sense.

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:32.660
The other two areas
where re-engineering

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:34.850
made complete sense
and was a no-brainer

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:37.700
was on the logging side
of components and data

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:38.550
engineering.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:42.727
I'll go into that
specifics in a bit.

00:15:42.727 --> 00:15:44.810
So if you look at all those
different components--

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:47.300
I said they were like almost
20 different components

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:50.660
and multiple subcomponents==
what I'm going to try to do

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:56.870
today, for you, is digest it all
and talk about these different

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.700
components or subcomponents
within these different levels.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:01.970
So networking applies
for all the components

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.270
that we're talking about.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:04.980
Let's start.

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:08.790
On a networking site,
on-prem, what we were running

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:11.580
were a lot of F5 load balancers.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:14.330
How many of you all have F5
load balances in your data

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:15.550
center today?

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:16.290
OK.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.620
As you guys probably know, those
things are great workhorses.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.250
They're extremely reliable.

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.120
They are extremely flexible.

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.610
You're not going to have that
same level of flexibility

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:27.480
in the cloud.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.310
And that was one
of the gaps that we

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.470
had to deal with, early on.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.560
Within our on-prem data
center, the F5 LTMs

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:38.470
were used for both external
and internal load balancing.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:40.600
They were also used for NAT.

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:45.690
So how did we migrate this
existing functionality to GCP?

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:49.440
We essentially ended up using
every type of load balancer

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.270
that's available in GCP.

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:55.370
We used the network load
balancer, the TCP Proxy Load

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:59.190
Balancer, HTTP load balancers,
and the internal load balancer.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.170
And I'll go through
why we ended up

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.560
needing to use every single
load balancer in a bit.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.030
The other aspect was NAT.

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:08.730
There was no immediate
NAT solution available.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:11.880
Managed NAT is still not
available within GCP today,

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.609
at least I don't think it is.

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:15.960
Or maybe it's just coming out.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:19.720
Two years ago, when we started,
it was not available yet.

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:24.329
So we ended up deploying
our own NAT solution.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:25.750
We insured it's HA.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:27.420
The good thing today
is, if you Google,

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.850
you will find several best
practice references on how

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:32.100
to set something like this up.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:34.400
This is a solved problem.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.350
At least we had to
roll our own back then.

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.030
And back then, we
weren't entirely sure

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:40.470
how to make sure that it was HA.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.750
So we had to figure that out.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:43.980
Other gotchas that
stuck out, when

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.080
we were doing the
networking piece, the first

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:47.610
was multi-cast.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:53.495
Again, if you're on-prem
and run physical networking,

00:17:53.495 --> 00:17:54.870
you're not going
to have problems

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:56.700
with having multi-cast.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:00.000
Multi-cast is not available
on any cloud provider.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.000
And we had components
that relied on multi-cast.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.020
In particular, some of
our Java applications

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.330
use this grid caching
technology called Infinispan.

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.970
The good news there is
changing Infinispan,

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.240
from using multi-cast, to
using other type of drivers--

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.130
we ended up using TCP Ping--
is pretty straightforward.

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:20.380
So we had to make that change.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:22.470
The other thing to be
aware of, especially

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.360
if you have a
large footprint, is

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:27.210
that there's a maximum cap
on the total amount of IPs

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:29.100
that you can have
within a network.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.880
And this catches
big deployments off.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:36.600
When we were doing this, we
had a maximum of 7,000 IPs.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.670
And this led to a lot of
sleepless nights of us

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:40.110
trying to work things out.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:44.040
The good news is, by the time
we actually went to production,

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.540
Google upgraded its network.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:48.210
And now, today, they
support up of 15,000 IPs.

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:53.180
And our 6,000-odd infrastructure
footprint fit within it.

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.520
The other gotcha was
around load balancers.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:58.760
And this is why we ended up
having multiple load balancers.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:01.340
The first gotcha was
we had a service that

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:05.520
was serving an endpoint,
publicly, on port 5061.

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:10.550
The TCP load balancer supports
a specific list of ports.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:14.630
There are about 12
ports that it support.

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:17.540
If you have existing
clients or services that

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.360
rely on these ports,
without changing it,

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:21.590
you're dead in the water.

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:23.340
You cannot serve from it.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.160
So that's why we
had to replace it

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:26.630
with a network load
balancer, which

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.460
didn't have this limitation.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:30.440
So now we have an NLB.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:34.100
Secondly, we had services
with TLS endpoints

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.100
where the clients
were doing mutual TLS.

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:40.700
So we couldn't rely, again,
on HTTPS load balancers

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:44.730
where TLS was being terminated
on the load balancer.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.810
We ended up solving this
by using TCP load balancers

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.230
and doing mutual TLS
on the instance itself.

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:53.530
Then we had a class
of applications,

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:56.990
with our bog standard
HTTPS endpoints,

00:19:56.990 --> 00:19:59.960
and we could serve them through
the HTTP load balancers.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.210
So these are some
of the limitations.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:04.760
And again, you look
at what's out here.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:08.420
You look at Kubernetes and
all this cool standard stuff.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:10.700
But we don't have that
luxury when you're

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:13.160
moving existing infrastructure.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:15.890
And finding these
type of problems early

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:19.770
and designing for
it is critical.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:23.190
On the storage side, the
story was a bit more simpler.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.380
The good news, the
summary or the upshot

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:31.740
was, for the most part, the
performance of Google's GCS

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:36.150
and PDSSD disks surpassed
what we were having

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:37.770
running in our data center.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:39.430
We had NetApp appliances.

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:43.200
And we had SAN arrays
built off SSDs.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:47.490
And both PDSSD and GCS was
a good fit and a replacement

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:50.050
for what was running there.

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:54.410
Now we get into the database
and caching side of things.

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:57.130
The first database that we
had to deal with were around--

00:20:57.130 --> 00:20:59.990
we had a large
Oracle deployment.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:02.770
Now, as some of you all might
know, if you've dug into this,

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:06.970
Oracle is not sanctioned
to run on GCP.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:10.780
Neither did we want to
pay any Larry Ellison tax.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:13.150
We think his yachts
are large enough.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:16.630
So not only did we have to
plan to migrate the database,

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:19.030
we had to plan to
transform the database,

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:22.690
off Oracle, to Postgres
before actually rolling it

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:24.740
out or migrating it to GCP.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:25.690
So we planned it in.

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:27.580
We got that done.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.800
When it came to some of
those mySQL databases,

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:33.130
we just targeted CloudSQL as
CloudSQL was reasonably mature

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:34.210
by that point.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:35.800
And not that many
of this database

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:39.910
was super critical for
production running of our app.

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:43.360
For Cassandra, a lot
of time was spent

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:46.030
trying to size the
instances that were going

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.900
to run the Cassandra nodes.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:53.140
So figuring out, from a CPU,
RAM, we ended up using PDSSD.

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:55.810
Turned out, those were fast
enough for our use case.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:57.730
We also spent a lot
of time working out

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:00.640
the operational aspects
of it, building playbooks,

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.250
understanding how we were
going to do rolling updates,

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:04.840
dealing with downed nodes.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:06.130
All that had to be set up.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.620
And we set up a special
team within our group

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:09.561
to handle that.

00:22:09.561 --> 00:22:11.810
They got most of it set up
within two to three months.

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:14.640
But that was a big investment.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.480
Redis and Memcached,
we started out

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:19.730
setting these up on our
own, doing something similar

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:20.660
to Cassandra.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:22.820
But then we found
a partner, Aiven.

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.322
And they are pretty competent.

00:22:24.322 --> 00:22:26.030
I would strongly
recommend them if you're

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.540
looking at trying to set
up something similar.

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:31.320
At that point, Memorystore
wasn't available.

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.040
We hope, going forward,
we can look at Memorystore

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:39.720
as a replacement for our third
party services from Aiven.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:46.050
Net-net, we ended up with
about 400 Cassandra nodes,

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:50.040
a host of mySQL, Postgres,
and Memcached instances.

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.710
The next portion was monitoring.

00:22:56.710 --> 00:22:59.190
And this is where
it got interesting.

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:06.090
What we had originally on-prem
was a large CA Wily deployment

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:10.320
capturing hundreds or
thousands of JMX metrics

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:12.230
from each instance.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:15.420
All of this was then
graphed on Grafana

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.664
and alerting was
done through Nagios.

00:23:17.664 --> 00:23:19.080
Now, when we first
looked at that,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.530
our natural inclination
was, you know what?

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:23.430
Let's move all of
this to Stackdriver.

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:24.330
That makes sense.

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:25.680
It's provided in Google Cloud.

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.300
Why wouldn't we leverage it?

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.610
Turns out there was a
big mismatch, at least

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.930
at that point, two
years ago, with getting

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:34.920
JMX metrics, especially the
vast amount of JMX metrics

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:37.890
that our services were
using, into Stackdriver.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.240
It was non-trivial.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.610
We would end up needing
to write lots of scripts,

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.800
manually listing
out the JMX metrics.

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.220
It wouldn't scale.

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:49.050
The second aspect that we
found Stackdriver suffered with

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:51.980
was just around the sheer
size of our dashboarding.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:54.090
The Stackdriver
dashboards back then--

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.970
I haven't looked at it lately,
so don't get upset with me--

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:00.600
but they just weren't
going to cut it.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:03.130
They were underperforming.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:04.170
They hung.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:07.660
The amount of analytics that you
could do on it was very poor.

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:10.230
So we end up going with
Datadog, and that's

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.570
worked out quite well for us.

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.460
So what we have,
today, is a setup

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.330
that looks similar to this.

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:21.480
We export sensitive metrics that
don't come from the instance,

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:25.020
like GLB metrics, metrics
from your managed services,

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:28.290
from your networking, from your
software defined networking.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:31.320
Those, we export from
Stackdriver to Datadog.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:35.580
Apart from that, we run the
Datadog agent on our instances.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:37.330
And that also pushes
metrics to Datadog.

00:24:37.330 --> 00:24:41.460
Datadog then functions as a
system to collect our metrics,

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:45.450
as well for us to build
handle alerting on it.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:48.120
These then become
available as dashboards,

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.720
which our ops team and
our developers use,

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.930
as well as for incident
management through OpsGenie.

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:57.120
It worked out, quite
well, for the most part.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:00.930
Today, we have over 6,000
hosts, running in this manner,

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:05.380
and over 250,000 metrics
running on Datadog.

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.070
So it's quite scalable.

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:09.480
There are a couple of
gotchas worth considering.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:14.920
Firstly, there was a lag between
capturing the Stackdriver

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.182
metrics to Datadog.

00:25:16.182 --> 00:25:17.640
Now, I don't know
if they were just

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:20.070
trying to throw a Spanner
into that integration-- just

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:20.790
kidding.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:22.680
But it was delayed.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:24.660
And initially, it was unusable.

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:27.150
There was a six-minute
delay between something

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:29.490
happening on the
load balances and it

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.230
being available to Datadog.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:34.300
We worked with Google and
Datadog to get that solved.

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:37.020
Today, the delay is
about a minute or less.

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:38.220
But there's still a delay.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.020
And we continue to
work with them on it.

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:42.300
If you guys are looking
at a similar integration,

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.270
do push them along.

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.276
It will benefit all of us.

00:25:45.276 --> 00:25:46.650
The other thing
worth considering

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:50.910
is that Datadog is not available
within the GCP infrastructure.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:52.710
At least, when we
rolled it out, it

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:55.350
was hosted on AWS,
which meant that you're

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:57.926
going to have a fair
bit of egress going out

00:25:57.926 --> 00:25:59.550
from your nodes,
especially if you have

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.440
a large enough
footprint like ours,

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.200
going to Datadog data
centers, which, I think,

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:08.850
now are AWS and
Europe and in US East.

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:11.220
However, the good news there
is the latencies introduced

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:12.570
by that wasn't too damaging.

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:14.917
For the most part, the
dashboards and the alerting

00:26:14.917 --> 00:26:16.500
and the metrics are
all being captured

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:17.760
in reasonable amount of time.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:19.110
And it's working OK for us.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:25.120
The next aspect
was around logging.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:28.330
And what we had
running on-prem was

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:32.770
we'd have instances running
a Kafka client that also

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:34.940
doubled up as a syslog agent.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.940
So applications would
just log as though they're

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.350
logging to syslog.

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:41.320
It would then get picked up by
a Kafka client, which would then

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:43.480
push it to a Kafka broker.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.130
And the Kafka broker
then had a bunch

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:47.660
of workers picking off
the logs and writing it

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:49.480
to a Hadoop file system.

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:50.480
This mostly worked.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:53.590
But what it meant is
that, to query these logs,

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:55.630
users would need to
spin up Hadoop jobs

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:58.860
and basically do a
MapReduce to find logs.

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:01.370
This was a bit slow and painful.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:02.950
So we wanted to re-engineer it.

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:05.230
We didn't want to just
have this coming along.

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:08.610
Secondly, also, especially if
you a replatforming on GCP,

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:12.190
there are tons of built
services, like Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:15.240
and Dataflow, that makes
sense to solve this problem.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.230
And that's what
we ended up with.

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:20.410
So what we ended up with is
we ran the standard Fluentd

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:22.940
agent on the instances.

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:26.950
This Fluentd agent acts
as a local rsyslog daemon.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:30.800
So again, you can have
those applications just

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:33.490
log as though they're
writing to syslog.

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.400
It then gets pushed to
Cloud Pub/Sub, which

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:38.680
then get picked up by Dataflow.

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:40.510
And then Dataflow
writes to two endpoints.

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:45.310
It writes to Elasticsearch
and is accessible to our team

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:46.570
through Kibana.

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.420
And it also writes
Avro files to GCS

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:52.180
and is accessible to
our teams from Zeppelin.

00:27:52.180 --> 00:27:59.350
The use cases for Elasticsearch
and our Zeppelin, Kibana

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:00.980
is different.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:04.710
Elasticsearch allows our users,
especially our operational team

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:06.460
or the developers
working on the services,

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.771
to access syslogs pretty quickly
and easily through some search

00:28:09.771 --> 00:28:10.270
queries.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:15.490
From a cost standpoint,
we try to keep

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.407
our Elasticsearch cluster
maintained at a fixed size.

00:28:18.407 --> 00:28:19.990
We don't want it to
continuously grow.

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:22.300
So what that means is
that we age out logs.

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.970
So we keep up, up to
about 14-days retention

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:26.950
within our
Elasticsearch clusters.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:31.804
But within GCS, we store those
logs, now, for perpetuity.

00:28:31.804 --> 00:28:33.970
But what it means is that,
to access data from that,

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:35.110
it's a bit more tedious.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:38.650
And we provide Zeppelin
as a front end for it.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:39.950
And it mostly works.

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:43.240
It also serves as the input
for a lot of our data analytics

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:47.870
and data engineering jobs, which
is what I'll talk about now.

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:51.830
So on a big data standpoint,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:55.720
we had raw data in HadoopFS.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.950
On-prem, we had a large
Hive-based data warehouse.

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:03.520
We were managing these data
engineering jobs using Oozie

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.990
and QlikView for dashboarding.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.840
This entire stack
was re-platformed.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:14.970
We replaced Oozie with
Airflow for job management.

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:21.300
We replaced all our
Hive data with Spark,

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:23.510
which would feed off GCS.

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:26.010
So the logs that we
had ingested earlier,

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:29.160
we also had event streaming
coming in through GCS.

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:31.680
And this would be picked
up by ETL jobs running

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:33.360
on Apache Spark.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:36.450
This Apache Spark cluster
would also write back to GCS,

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:37.350
where necessary.

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:41.010
And for our data science
and data engineering work,

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:44.030
we would use Parquet files.

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:46.780
We would also sometimes,
primarily for business analysis

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:50.160
and for business
reporting, run ETL jobs

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:52.040
and write it
straight to BigQuery.

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:55.880
And then we'd have Tableau run
queries against our BigQuery

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:56.630
instance.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:58.190
So this allowed us to--

00:29:58.190 --> 00:30:01.730
in some cases, when you have
clear business reports that

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:04.490
are clearly defined and not
going to change too much,

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:06.200
we have ETL jobs
write to BigQuery

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:09.260
and make them queryable,
extremely quickly.

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:12.680
In cases where we want to
provide more ad hoc analytics,

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:14.570
we've provided that
through Zeppelin

00:30:14.570 --> 00:30:17.470
running over our Parquet
files within GCS.

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:19.970
This, as well, has
worked quite well for us.

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:25.760
We migrated over 100 ETL jobs
that, today, run over 18,000

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:26.920
vCPUs.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.870
We process 20 terabytes
of data a day.

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:33.860
And these jobs, on average, run
between one to two hours max.

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:36.110
In the past, when we
were running on-prem,

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:38.660
these jobs would take
sometimes up to two days.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:42.820
So this has been a huge
improvement for the business.

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:45.000
And finally, at the
top of the stack

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.830
is our application service.

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.800
And for this, when we
looked at what was running,

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:53.130
initially, we saw a combination
of over 15 different type

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:56.520
of CPU, RAM, disk combinations.

00:30:56.520 --> 00:30:58.740
We tried initially trying
to streamline this,

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:00.360
migrating what we had.

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:04.380
But what we found, in the end,
is that, at least for our use

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:08.640
cases, the configurations
that were running on-prem,

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:10.620
were not the configurations
that we ended up

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:12.210
running in the cloud.

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:13.980
In retrospect, it seems obvious.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:17.010
Because we tried to autoscale
some of these instances, what

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.020
we could end up with was
we generally ended up

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:20.730
reducing the size
of the footprint

00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:23.400
of these individual instances,
so that we could scale them

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.200
up and down easily.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:27.840
So my suggestion,
here, the takeaway

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.847
here would be don't waste too
much time trying to do this,

00:31:30.847 --> 00:31:33.180
especially if you're coming
from an on-prem environment.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:36.060
Just get to the point that you
can test this, and figure it

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:37.380
out as you go along.

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:41.690
How you do that is what I'm
going to touch on a bit later.

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:45.419
So we've just finished
the whirlwind plan

00:31:45.419 --> 00:31:47.710
of trying to figure out how
we're going to re-engineer,

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:50.860
what we're going to engineer,
how we're going to run that.

00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:54.310
That, now again, took
us about two months-plus

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:57.880
sitting in windowless
meeting rooms,

00:31:57.880 --> 00:31:59.320
working together
through partners,

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:02.230
like Pythian and CloudCover
and our engineering team,

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:04.030
and getting all that
bashed out, testing

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:05.770
some of the assumptions.

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:07.540
But now that we
had figured it out,

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:10.630
the next step was to actually
build the service up.

00:32:10.630 --> 00:32:15.790
Now that we know where we want
to go, how do we get running?

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:19.420
The first thing that we did,
before actually coming up with

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:21.550
a plan was-- and this was
actually done quite early

00:32:21.550 --> 00:32:23.620
in our transition plan--

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:26.050
is that we started
with some guiding

00:32:26.050 --> 00:32:29.410
principles as to how we were
going to do traffic cutover.

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:32.044
And again, I think this
is a takeaway for teams.

00:32:32.044 --> 00:32:33.460
You need to sit
with your business

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:36.880
and figure out what
is acceptable to them.

00:32:36.880 --> 00:32:40.240
From our end, because we are
real-time messaging system,

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:44.530
with tens of millions of users,
it was decided that we cannot

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:47.620
afford to have any
downtime for our users.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:50.650
I put an asterisk there, and
I'll come back to it in a bit.

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:53.470
But we didn't want to have
any downtime for our users.

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:55.360
We couldn't afford that.

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:57.640
What that meant, though,
is that, from a planning

00:32:57.640 --> 00:33:01.570
standpoint, from a timeline
standpoint, we buffered things.

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:02.660
We stretched things out.

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:03.910
We ensured that
we had enough time

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:05.368
to do the engineering,
which is why

00:33:05.368 --> 00:33:06.700
it took two years in the end.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:08.825
There is definitely going
to be a trade-ff in terms

00:33:08.825 --> 00:33:10.870
of how much downtime
you're willing to take,

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:13.480
and how much time the overall
transition is going to take.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:16.952
That, you need to figure out
yourself, but do consider this.

00:33:16.952 --> 00:33:18.910
The second thing that we
knew, from the get go,

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:22.390
is that we wanted to allow for
learning through the transition

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:25.317
process, through the
traffic cutover process.

00:33:25.317 --> 00:33:27.400
Because, as I mentioned
earlier for the most part,

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:29.800
this was a team that
did not build or operate

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:31.630
this service before.

00:33:31.630 --> 00:33:34.840
We knew that there were
lot of unknown unknowns.

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:37.150
And the only way to
figure these unknowns

00:33:37.150 --> 00:33:39.340
was to actually
experience it when

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:41.290
we were running in production.

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:42.790
How did we achieve that?

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:45.370
We achieved that by ensuring
that the traffic cutover could

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:47.560
be done in an
incremental fashion.

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:50.540
And I'll touch on how we
achieved that in a bit.

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:54.310
The last thing is we wanted
to be resilient to mistakes.

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:57.670
We wanted to make sure that, if
we had a problem in the traffic

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:01.210
cutover, we didn't
have to incur downtime

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.070
until the problem was fixed.

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:05.590
We needed a way that we
could send traffic back

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:08.530
to our on-prem DC, and then
ensure that services could

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:13.210
continue to run while we figured
out how to actually fix it

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.520
in Google Cloud.

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:17.530
So that meant no big bangs.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:20.370
We couldn't afford them.

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:24.449
The traffic cutover, itself, can
be broken down to four stages.

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:26.850
Firstly, we're setting
up joint networking.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:30.030
Secondly, is getting the data
replicated, the appserver

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.949
deployment, and the client
traffic cutover itself.

00:34:32.949 --> 00:34:35.377
Let's go through this.

00:34:35.377 --> 00:34:37.710
One thing that's really nice
about Google Cloud, that we

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:39.750
leverage, was the
ability to set up

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:43.440
a dedicated interconnect
between our on-prem DC

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:46.850
and the data Centers in Asia.

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:50.670
Now, because Google has
a private interconnect--

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:53.489
they talk about it, it's
called their Premium network--

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:54.929
this ensured that
we could set up

00:34:54.929 --> 00:34:56.969
these networks
between these DCs,

00:34:56.969 --> 00:35:00.450
and they were low latency
and highly reliable.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.960
We didn't have any outage,
any service impacting outage,

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:04.740
during the period.

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:06.490
Every now and then,
we'd have some spikes,

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:07.800
but they were mostly resolved.

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:09.660
But most importantly,
the latency

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:12.570
between our data centers
in Asia, back to Canada,

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:15.670
was between 200,
250 milliseconds.

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:18.930
This meant that we could do a
certain type of traffic cutover

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:24.840
strategies that not having this
type of latency would afford.

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:27.240
So, for example, if the
latencies were a lot higher,

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:29.900
some of the database replication
techniques wouldn't work.

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:33.530
I'll touch on that a bit.

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:37.700
So because a large
footprint of our data

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:42.470
was stored in Cassandra, that
got us replication for free.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.700
Cassandra, as some of
you guys probably know,

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:46.580
has native replication
built into it.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:48.200
So that was pretty
straightforward.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:49.940
We spun up our
Cassandra nodes, let

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:53.300
the replication run through,
and we had our data in GCP.

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:56.960
When it came to Postgres, we set
up master-slave replications.

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:57.770
Alvaro is here.

00:35:57.770 --> 00:36:00.150
He worked with us on it.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:02.060
And what we had
to do, in the end,

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:05.090
to actually get the users
onto the new master,

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:07.120
was to actually do
a master promotion.

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:10.940
Now, this is the one time that
we had to take some downtime.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:12.830
When we made the
master promotion,

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:16.490
we then have the application
service point to the new master

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:19.172
and proxy request back from
the old application service.

00:36:19.172 --> 00:36:20.630
So there was some
minimal downtime,

00:36:20.630 --> 00:36:23.780
of about 5 to 10 minutes,
while this process was ongoing.

00:36:23.780 --> 00:36:26.420
This didn't impact any of
our key services, though.

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:27.650
Messages continued to work.

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:29.210
People could chat
with each other.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:31.460
And therefore, it was
seen as acceptable.

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:34.242
When it came to caches, we
modified our application

00:36:34.242 --> 00:36:35.450
to be able to do dual writes.

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:38.180
So any application that
would use Memcached or Redis,

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:41.170
we introduced the ability to
define more than one cluster.

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:43.890
It would write to both
clusters, one cluster operating

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:45.890
in GCP, the other on-prem.

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:47.456
And then, when we
finally cutover,

00:36:47.456 --> 00:36:49.580
we would both read and
write from the next cluster.

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:56.060
So we have the
networking set up.

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:57.490
We've got the data migrated.

00:36:57.490 --> 00:37:01.010
The next step was actually
rolling out the appservers.

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:03.010
Now, if you remember
from the start,

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:05.380
I mentioned there were
many, many appservers,

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:06.680
many, many services.

00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:09.370
So the first step
was to really map out

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:11.830
all the dependencies, with
the application servers,

00:37:11.830 --> 00:37:14.710
and pick one application
server with the least

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:16.390
amount of dependencies.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:19.390
We then would take the
deployment of that application

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:21.202
server till the end.

00:37:21.202 --> 00:37:22.660
What I mean by
"till the end" is we

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.840
would have production traffic
working on that one application

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:29.080
server, that one service,
before moving to the next.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:32.230
This is almost like canarying
your traffic cutover.

00:37:32.230 --> 00:37:34.810
It allowed us to ensure
that any type of mistakes

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:36.910
that we might have made
with our first service,

00:37:36.910 --> 00:37:39.457
we did not repeat for
subsequent services

00:37:39.457 --> 00:37:40.540
that we were cutting over.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:44.050
And I think this was
very useful for us.

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:49.060
So when we moved the
service over to GCP,

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:52.360
if there were any requests that
were dependent on services that

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:54.670
weren't in GCP yet,
because we are cutting

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.950
one service at a
time, again, we would

00:37:56.950 --> 00:38:00.280
be able to leverage our
GCP direct interconnect

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:04.810
to just proxy this request back
to our on-prem data center.

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:07.330
If we didn't have
that low latency link,

00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:09.730
a lot of these type of
migration techniques

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:12.040
wouldn't be possible.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:15.010
Once we had the single,
individual appserver running

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:19.990
in GCP, we would then cut server
to server traffic to it first.

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:22.540
And again, that also would
be cut incrementally.

00:38:22.540 --> 00:38:25.360
We'd have 10% of the
fleet make the requests,

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:27.940
and then slowly bump
it up more as we

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:30.770
had more assurance that the
service was running fine.

00:38:36.790 --> 00:38:39.950
We then moved to the
client traffic cutover.

00:38:39.950 --> 00:38:41.810
How did we do this
incrementally?

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:45.410
The first step required
instrumenting the client

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:49.670
to be able to receive service
endpoints from a third party,

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:53.120
so basically push the
service endpoints to it.

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:55.010
The way we achieved
this was, obviously,

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:57.920
we had to push out a new
version of a client that

00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:59.570
supported this capability.

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:02.420
But we also leveraged
Firebase Remote Config,

00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:05.390
as the back end service, to push
these values or these service

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:07.190
endpoints to the client.

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:10.430
We could have built our
own service to do this,

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:12.860
but the benefit of using
Firebase Remote Config

00:39:12.860 --> 00:39:16.850
was that it already has internal
capabilities around client

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:20.060
targeting that proved
to be very useful,

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:24.920
and it also allows you to
roll values of key updates

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:26.330
incrementally.

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:30.200
So we could say 1% of the
entire client population

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:33.800
has this new service endpoint,
see how the service performs,

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:35.870
and then slowly ramp this up.

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:39.632
When we got to 100%, we'd
then move to the next service.

00:39:39.632 --> 00:39:41.090
So essentially,
that's what it was.

00:39:41.090 --> 00:39:43.830
We did service by
service, piece by piece.

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:47.330
We take the learnings and
then apply to the next one.

00:39:47.330 --> 00:39:49.910
You repeat it 20
times, and you fully

00:39:49.910 --> 00:39:52.040
cutover without
your users knowing.

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:55.680
And that's it.

00:39:55.680 --> 00:40:00.219
That's how we migrated the
entire BBM service to GCP.

00:40:00.219 --> 00:40:02.010
I think there are a
couple of key learnings

00:40:02.010 --> 00:40:04.410
that we discovered
as we went along.

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:08.070
Firstly, is there are
definitely going to be gotchas.

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:11.130
Unless you are deploying a
Cloud-native service that

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.060
was already designed
for the Cloud,

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:14.970
there are gotchas
hiding somewhere.

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:16.230
And you need to dig them out.

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.090
If you haven't
found them yet, it's

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:20.730
probably because you
haven't looked hard enough.

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:24.630
Get alignment from the business
in terms of what impact

00:40:24.630 --> 00:40:27.450
they're willing to take
as part of the transition.

00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:31.170
This will really define how
much effort and time and money

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:34.802
you're going to need to
invest into your transition.

00:40:34.802 --> 00:40:37.260
The third aspect is-- and this
is something that we totally

00:40:37.260 --> 00:40:38.910
missed when we
were starting out--

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:42.150
the communications, between
the different teams involved

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:43.920
in the transition,
is going to be

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:46.710
your single, toughest,
non-technical problem.

00:40:46.710 --> 00:40:48.390
We were not prepared for this.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.590
This communication can
be between any group.

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:54.360
It could be between your
partners, yourself, and Google

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:55.980
Cloud technical team.

00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:58.890
Or in our case, for example,
it was that, and also

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:01.440
the combinatorial
impact of having teams

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:05.070
spread across Canada,
Singapore, and Jakarta.

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:06.810
You multiply all
this, and you've

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:10.770
got a very n square
problem on your hand.

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:12.600
Lastly, I'd say--
and this really

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:15.960
worked well for us-- is
bake-in the ability to fail.

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:17.850
You're going to be
making mistakes.

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:21.360
That's the only way you're going
to get something complex right.

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:23.040
If you bake it in
from the start,

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:25.320
you can recover,
learn, and move on.

00:41:28.257 --> 00:41:29.090
Thank you very much.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:51.387
[MUSIC PLAYING]

